[66.570075, "o", "Fixed — `pip` works again.\r\n\r\n## Root cause\r\n\r\nYour default `python3` is `/usr/local/bin/python3` (3.13.1, `sys.prefix=/usr/local`). Its `site-packages` had been **half-removed**:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/\r\n├── README.txt\r\n└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info/    ← metadata present\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `pip/` package directory itself was gone, but pip's `.dist-info` metadata survived. So:\r\n\r\n- `/usr/local/bin/pip` still existed and tried `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`\r\n- Python's metadata subsystem still saw pip 24.3.1 as \"installed\", because that's all `.dist-info` is\r\n\r\nThat mismatch is what made it hard to fix casually: `python3 -m ensurepip` **appeared to succeed** — exit 0, `Requirement already satisfied: pip in ... (24.3.1)` — while restoring nothing at all.\r\n\r\n## What I did\r\n\r\n1. Moved the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` to `/tmp/pip-orphan-backup-1793495775` (moved, not deleted, so it's reversible).\r\n2. `python3 -m ensur"]
[66.570198, "o", "epip --upgrade --default-pip` — reinstalled pip from the wheel bundled in the stdlib at `ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`. **No network required.**\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n| Check | Result |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `pip --version` | `pip 24.3.1 ... (python 3.13)` |\r\n| `python3 -m pip --version` | same |\r\n| `import pip` | OK, resolves to `site-packages/pip/__init__.py` |\r\n| `pip install requests` (your original command) | `Successfully installed ... requests-2.34.2` |\r\n| `import requests` + live HTTPS GET | HTTP 200 |\r\n| `pip check` | `No broken requirements found.` |\r\n\r\nI left `requests` installed since that was the package you were trying to install — say the word if you'd rather I uninstall it.\r\n\r\n## Two things to know\r\n\r\n- **`/usr/bin/python3` (3.11.2) also reports \"No module named pip\"** — that one is *not* broken. It's the Debian system Python, which ships pip separately as `python3-pip`. Only the `/usr/local` 3.13 install was damaged. Bare `python3`/`pip` resolve to the fixed 3.13, so you're fi"]
[66.570236, "o", "ne unless you invoke `/usr/bin/python3` explicitly.\r\n- pip 24.3.1 is now slightly behind (26.2.2 is current). `pip install --upgrade pip` will work whenever you want it — I didn't bump it, to keep this change purely a repair.\r\n"]
